nodecode-opencode-shepherd is a rewrite of okazakee's okazakee/opencode-shepherd:

  https://github.com/okazakee/opencode-shepherd
  commit 207a1443a5daa20e20824d13776794c0057cd539
  MIT, Copyright (c) 2026 okazakee (LICENSE)

What was rewritten: the Python CLI and its opencode v2 client became Common
Lisp in one Nodecode cell -- the same HTTP surface (`/session`,
`/session/active`, `/session/<id>/message|form|permission|prompt|interrupt`,
`/shell/<id>`), the same read-only snapshot of a server's sessions on which
the alert decision rests (active flag, updated and idle times, pending
questions and permissions, the last message and its outcome, and the
background shells that will resume a turn), and the same conservatism about
what counts as waiting: a turn waiting on a background shell is not reported,
and a sub-session never reports a finished turn because its parent does. The
CLI's subcommands became seven model-facing tools -- opencode_sessions,
opencode_waiting, opencode_tail, opencode_send, opencode_permission,
opencode_form, opencode_interrupt -- plus the /opencode command, with
sessions resolved by id or by a fragment of an id or title exactly as
`resolve` did, and with the same deduplication of what was already said: by
form and permission id, and by idle timestamp and outcome, over a first poll
that seeds the existing turns and a fresh-grace window that keeps a turn
which resumes by itself from being news.

What was left out, and why: the parts that existed to reach a person who was
not at the machine -- the pipx install of the CLI from a pinned git commit,
the cron and systemd wiring, the snapshot piped over SSH, the Telegram bot
and the notify-command sink, and the CLI's own config file of
enabled/quiet-hours/reminder settings. Here the watch is a worker in the
agent's own process (`poll_seconds` in the section), and what it finds stands
as one notice the model reads in its prompt and the person sees as a toast,
so the reminder clock and the re-enable recap have nothing left to do. The
event-driven mode (`--events`, an SSE connection to the server's bus) is not
reproduced either: the poll it falls back to is the default here. Nor does
the cell make or delete sessions (`new`, `close`): a session is the person's,
and the cell only reads it and answers it.
